Backport of https://github.com/silnrsi/smith/pull/87, filed 2026-10-02 and
not merged yet: look up the programs that only the built-in test commands
need with mandatory=False, so `smith configure` succeeds without them.
Related: https://github.com/silnrsi/smith/issues/86 asks upstream to tag a
release, which would also let this package follow tags again.

smith registers its test commands in FontTests.__init__ regardless of what
the wscript asks for, and every TestCommand reports its tools through
get_build_tools: `sile` and `sileftml` carry extracmds=['sile'], `test` and
`xtest` carry ['cmptxtrender'], and the base class adds 'cp' and 'ttftable'.
package.py's subconfig collects all of those into one set and calls
find_program on each without mandatory=False, so a missing one aborts
configure. The result is that `smith configure` fails with "Could not find
the program sile" on any font project, including SIL's own font-padauk and
font-abyssinica, unless sile, palaso-python's cmptxtrender and Font::TTF's
ttftable are all installed, even when the wscript never runs those tests.

The headline test tools are already optional in the same function (xetex,
ots-sanitize, fontbakery, fontspector, diffenator2), and the missing tool is
reported again, with its own message, when a test that needs it runs. This
makes the test commands' extra tools behave the same way. Build tools stay
mandatory: those are reported by font.py and keyboard.py through
Package.get_build_tools and are untouched here, so a wscript that needs
fontmake or psfufo2ttf still fails at configure time if it is missing.

--- a/src/smithlib/font_tests.py
+++ b/src/smithlib/font_tests.py
@@ -207,7 +207,14 @@
         for ts in list(self._allTests.values()) :
             for t in ts :
                 res.update(t.get_build_tools(ctx))
-        return res
+        # The built-in test commands are registered whether or not the
+        # wscript uses them, so their tools must not be mandatory at
+        # configure time; each test reports its own missing tool when it
+        # runs. Build tools come from Package.get_build_tools and stay
+        # mandatory.
+        for prog in res :
+            ctx.find_program(prog, var=prog.upper(), mandatory=False)
+        return set()
 
 
 class TestFile(object) :
